{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/check-wasm.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-14T12:35:23.251071Z","id":"7e91434f9bd90b956ab7c5a17a3dc20736797b9733de4fad82e796ac55075b46","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"No longer true: scripts/quality_gates.sh:158 runs scripts/check-wasm.sh (the thread's close, cc73bc73), and the script now also checks clax-content-git (scripts/check-wasm.sh:2-4).","supersedes":"f7a7babd97c31991fc6c0cffaff7a09bd20a7883e38d1a84d0622e6c162e102d","tags":["reason:obsolete","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/perf-clients.py","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T04:47:23.153271Z","id":"d5bf7790d56e2ef77e9188e4f838271a7fa7c2df25d43e72e2f571340e7ea94a","body":{"detail":"The measure is the median, over slow_rounds floods of flood_batches document batches, of the daemon's footprint growth (budget 1024 KB). Measured medians were 0-32 KB per flood with and without CPU load, against 320-448 KB for the earlier single-flood RSS measure. A build that keeps a slow client's whole backlog (QUEUE in crates/clax-server/src/stream.rs raised from 64 to 1<<20) was built but never measured: its first run stalled in macOS's assessment of new executables. That a kept backlog exceeds the budget is inferred from its size (5000 events with 160-character paths per flood), not observed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":827},"end":{"line":837},"content_hash":"be67e806b167263d6cedfacadd444a8750ddaa218457c63c4f32d442b666ac9e"},"suggested_fix":"Run scripts/perf-clients.py --quick once against that variant binary and confirm slow_growth_kb fails.","summary":"slow_growth_kb has not been shown to catch a kept backlog","supersedes":"2fae41b92c5d2a7666d11c3a6fb18f6e96b7466d6d9f7db468f0e8e22cfe2922","tags":["review","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/npm-ci-stamped.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T06:21:31.835008Z","id":"1bbd149ecbccd48cd976630f7121239641fe9b92fd8daef0106c6198c35cba74","body":{"detail":"web/ and plugins/pi/ moved to pnpm (scripts/pnpm-install-stamped.sh, pnpm-lock.yaml); crates/clax-worker kept npm and its package-lock.json, so this stamped npm ci stays for it alone (scripts/quality_gates.sh lane_worker, just worker-test). Moving it means a pnpm-lock.yaml for wrangler and its workerd and esbuild install scripts, which pnpm runs only when allowBuilds lists them (web/pnpm-workspace.yaml allows none), and an unchanged deploy path for crates/clax-worker/deploy.mjs and scripts/cloudflare-setup.sh, which run npm.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":2},"end":{"line":3},"content_hash":"678ec3cbf9e49427879ba05b2fcb42a99778769e4fe186058dabbab2aa3ac421"},"summary":"crates/clax-worker installs with pnpm, as web/ and plugins/pi/ do","tags":["build","revisit:the Worker's packages come under Bazel, or npm leaves the machines that run the gates","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/check-wasm.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T14:41:35.311207Z","id":"93e0f83d91a850717ad4f58f21482ec4ec4b9bbc14fdb94652f95d2c10285326","body":{"detail":".github/workflows/ci.yml and rust-toolchain.toml add the wasm32-unknown-unknown target, and the script now also tries Debian and Ubuntu's /usr/lib/llvm-<N>/bin. Whether ubuntu-latest has a clang there that targets wasm32 beside an llvm-ar was not checked; the first CI run of the gates shows it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":30},"end":{"line":42},"content_hash":"e66b7d02889242a086cff19ef6e715715aeb07f55cc86dd9f77faecf1ca7e1c7"},"summary":"The wasm32 lane has run on macOS only: CI's Ubuntu LLVM is unverified","supersedes":"66e95d2ea22782cbc37390be298fd766ce5ef445f0e65031e13510713967d590","tags":["gates","ci","wasm","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T14:41:35.341823Z","id":"69fa4332dd9c3d30ee14a84f1597e6546f828db881567aabbc837c3938bfbdd7","body":{"detail":"They run first, alone, because several were timing-sensitive. Since 5da6c9a the view-mounting and child-process tests run under limits that end hangs only (MOUNT_TIMEOUT_MS 30 s, CHILD_TIMEOUT_MS 60 s, web/shell/src/test/timeouts.ts), so moving them into the lanes would save about 9 s of a gates run.","kind":"suggestion","ref":"git:e01d4166","span":{"start":{"line":162},"end":{"line":168},"content_hash":"82fe20109f02bee76696788391627a4839b255388a7a3efecad0187f317922f8"},"summary":"The shell's unit tests run alone (about 9 s); they may now join the lanes","supersedes":"0256afa979a18b3e310fc022b681eab3bb8d4f5157de3ae88f505bef6746877c","tags":["gate-speed","session:claude-code:4661063b-b304-434f-95f6-59a30e211068","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/check-crate-layers.py","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T14:52:20.537149Z","id":"904f8fe2617a660a7b40d8ce6a99608d286b2a72ba8c9ba0409327bf9486ffff","body":{"detail":"The layout's picture (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-16-workspace-layout-design.md section 2.1, lines 184-188) and ALLOWED here gain the crates the hosted work added: clax-rt (the runtime seam, tokio natively and a Worker's event loop on wasm32) depends on no workspace crate and every daemon crate uses it, so it joins clax-core's set; clax-git depends on nothing; clax-content-git on clax-core, clax-rt and clax-git. crates/clax-worker stays outside the workspace. This amends the decided layout spec, which is the owner's.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":31},"end":{"line":48},"content_hash":"8239201ac2a10212769d2e0e165229b85b50b75ec65a63159416546a23401147"},"summary":"Place clax-rt beside clax-core, clax-git on its own and clax-content-git over both in the layout","tags":["layout","status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/perf-clients.py","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T22:55:11.514108Z","id":"cede54937cd346ae19c60a8ab7e67c824d62cb677f6b17313455613849061ff3","body":{"detail":"The measure is the median, over slow_rounds floods of flood_batches document batches, of the daemon's footprint growth (budget 1024 KB). Measured medians were 0-32 KB per flood with and without CPU load, against 320-448 KB for the earlier single-flood RSS measure. A build that keeps a slow client's whole backlog (QUEUE in crates/clax-server/src/stream.rs raised from 64 to 1<<20) was built but never measured: its first run stalled in macOS's assessment of new executables. That a kept backlog exceeds the budget is inferred from its size (5000 events with 160-character paths per flood), not observed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":829},"end":{"line":839},"content_hash":"be67e806b167263d6cedfacadd444a8750ddaa218457c63c4f32d442b666ac9e"},"suggested_fix":"Run scripts/perf-clients.py --quick once against that variant binary and confirm slow_growth_kb fails.","summary":"slow_growth_kb has not been shown to catch a kept backlog","supersedes":"d5bf7790d56e2ef77e9188e4f838271a7fa7c2df25d43e72e2f571340e7ea94a","tags":["review","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-17T22:55:11.546191Z","id":"19ed076eac262bec12a035436c05705e62de550b5728608bfe916336fa552b44","body":{"detail":"test-repos.mjs needs jj (CLAX_TEST_JJ or jj on PATH) and fails without it; no gate installs one. After commit cfe9fdaa it passes with CLAX_TEST_JJ set (node test-repos.mjs --no-build: every check ok). The e2e lane's node e2e.mjs now publishes into git through the stand-in (REPO_STORE=memory) but does not clone.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":140},"end":{"line":147},"content_hash":"b307cd78ca40244b0f066e84429c5be8f1d9fc44367795b203ac88b7d8c11c78"},"summary":"The worker lane does not run crates/clax-worker/test-repos.mjs (the RepoDO stand-in, /git/ clones and pushes, R2 assets)","supersedes":"b186353e057e2a4c353cbab7a1da788a980a4c83430c17d1f2d2dcb722ea6f3b","tags":["worker","gates","session:claude-code:6847a238-376f-486d-8e40-4d0fa676e347"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/crate-package.py","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T03:22:35.072654Z","id":"54f2cebff7455dc8dd70dfaefffba283d87f7ff21b1166c392264ac987cb2e35","body":{"detail":"Not taken: it makes about eleven internal crates (clax-core, clax-store, clax-server, ...) public crates.io names that others can depend on, each release has to publish them in layer order with versioned path dependencies, and clax-embedded would still need its web UI, extension and plugins copied inside its own directory at publish time. The generated package keeps every internal crate private at the cost of rewriting paths (rewrite_sources, lines 211-232).","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":2},"end":{"line":38},"content_hash":"853c6518c8dbe0dbd3c800cd1bed9b5cb258f83c6d6ddd0c64245866ec5564fe"},"summary":"Publish every workspace crate to crates.io instead of one generated clax package","tags":["revisit:a workspace crate is wanted as a library on crates.io, or the path rewrite in scripts/crate-package.py breaks on a source pattern it cannot handle","session:claude-code:b2fe0abc-990b-4628-b4c7-4c6d7f3f261b"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/crate-package.py","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T03:22:35.110063Z","id":"24303270533236998727e5f7ca681eed8273d90551f9ca9be3237987f3c06117","body":{"detail":"rewrite_sources replaces every crate:: in a bundled library crate's sources with crate::<module>:: and every clax_<name>:: with crate::clax_<name>::, comments and string literals included. True of the sources at this commit (git grep finds none of these patterns in the eleven bundled crates' src/). A #[macro_export] macro would move to the package root and break its crate::<module>:: callers; a crate feature would be dropped (merged_dependencies refuses optional dependencies); a string such as a serde 'with = \"crate::x\"' is rewritten, which is right, but a log or error message quoting a path would change. tracing targets already read clax::clax_server::... in the package instead of clax_server::... . just crate-package (cargo check --locked of the packed crate) catches whatever does not compile.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":211},"end":{"line":232},"content_hash":"10cea40f7e1f818dfdb6c107a712446c3b8fb861b56b0d468eb7956b1cdb7ce0"},"summary":"The package's module rewrite is textual and assumes the bundled crates have no #[macro_export] macros, no extern crate, no crate features and no strings naming crate paths","tags":["session:claude-code:b2fe0abc-990b-4628-b4c7-4c6d7f3f261b"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/run.test.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T15:58:57.162111Z","id":"f4b54e1e9ce4bdb91e2150c8f4250d18f87f175b52d7c6e23b93120668c34c47","body":{"detail":"Failure: on any Linux runner (CI's ubuntu-latest, .github/workflows/ci.yml:9), `stat -f %l FILE` is GNU stat's --file-system mode, where %l is the maximum file name length (255), and it exits 0, so the `|| stat -c %h` fallback never runs. links is \"255\", `[ \"$links\" = 1 ]` is false, and the check \"web: copies, writable, one link each\" fails though run.sh's _copy_dist made single-link copies. CI run 39266520838 on main (2026-10-19) shows exactly this: `FAIL: //scripts:run_choice` with `FAIL: web: copies, writable, one link each` while every other run_choice check passed. GNU's format sequences for -f: https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/stat-invocation.html. scripts/test-extension-signing.sh:104 and scripts/test-dev.sh:48 order the two the safe way (GNU `-c` first, which BSD stat rejects).","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":228},"end":{"line":228},"content_hash":"72f97b8c6d1d42bf5ac76e214f90e9ed98a8811cd5df603ec9ac0a053dc99ab4"},"suggested_fix":"Try GNU first: `links=\"$(stat -c %h \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || stat -f %l \"$f\")\"` (BSD stat has no -c, so it falls through), or use `find \"$f\" -links 1` which is the same on both.","summary":"The link-count probe tries BSD `stat -f %l` first, which GNU stat accepts as a file-system query, so //scripts:run_choice fails on Linux","tags":["review","review:deep-dive-2026-10-19","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T15:58:57.162295Z","id":"8b67a515a0421d516a1342d690291a6fd323926418909f08f26d10cfcb657491","body":{"detail":"When the lock's recorded PID is dead, each waiting run does `rm -rf \"$LOCK\"; continue`. Runs A and B both read the dead PID; A removes the lock, mkdirs a new one and writes its PID; B, still acting on the dead PID it read, then runs `rm -rf \"$LOCK\"`, deleting A's lock, and mkdirs its own. Both now run the gates in one checkout, which the comment at lines 19-21 says corrupts both (one `pnpm install` replaces node_modules under the other's tests). A run killed between `mkdir` (line 23) and `echo $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"` (line 31) also leaves a lock with no PID, which every later run waits on forever (an empty holder is never treated as stale).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":22},"end":{"line":31},"content_hash":"9a092ae504f4657e6be4d49017d31998aad7699519204633339d362b82c7034c"},"suggested_fix":"Take over a stale lock by atomically renaming it aside (`mv \"$LOCK\" \"$LOCK.stale.$$\"` succeeds for one run only) and re-read the PID from the moved directory before removing it; or hold the lock with `flock` on a file (util-linux, `brew install flock`) / `shlock` on macOS. Treat a PID-less lock older than a minute as stale.","summary":"Two runs that both find a stale lock can both remove it, so the second deletes the first's fresh lock and both run in one checkout","tags":["review","review:deep-dive-2026-10-19","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T15:58:57.162327Z","id":"ad18e90298d2d15379dc6f64ad4dcadc388175aaeda341efba964d6a0639b0e5","body":{"detail":"AGENTS.md (\"Code\") says tests are load-tolerant with no wall-clock budgets in any check. lane_e2e awaits tests-built, linted and release-built (lines 94-96), and lane_pi and lane_binary await clax-built; each await counts 9000 iterations of `sleep 0.2` and then prints \"gave up waiting\" and fails the lane, even though the lane it waits on is still running and will mark it. On a cold cargo cache under load (every lane compiling at once into one target/), the debug test build or the release build can exceed 30 minutes, and the run then fails for time alone. The cap is also unnecessary: `await` already returns when the awaited lane's `.done` file appears without the mark (lines 255-258), so a failed or killed lane cannot leave it waiting forever.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":250},"end":{"line":263},"content_hash":"19fd9a7beed7c7f6152436899aac5ca4205e7398bfc392aa98f9b0d164618c05"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the iteration cap and rely on the `.done` check (a lane always touches `.done` on exit via its EXIT trap, line 243); if a backstop is wanted, wait on the awaited lane's PID being alive instead of a count.","summary":"`await` gives up after 9000 polls (about 30 minutes), a wall-clock budget that fails the run on a cold or loaded machine","tags":["review","review:deep-dive-2026-10-19","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/run.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T15:58:57.162369Z","id":"7c6ad22aa8176cb7d53106c9502f00c016fdc89c6e42689834d3a72ba02d309b","body":{"detail":"scripts/bazel-test.sh:208-213 lints every crates/*/clippy.toml crate a second time with its own clippy.toml, and run.sh's lint hard-codes `-//crates/clax-commands/...` and `//crates/clax-commands:clippy.toml`. Today only crates/clax-commands/clippy.toml exists, so they agree; when a second crate gains a clippy.toml, `just lint` lints it with the root clippy.toml only and passes code that `just ci` and CI reject.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":146},"end":{"line":152},"content_hash":"68ec81302306d8391c6b96afbc532139bb3088a67067c3ef6d8bbd1032b600b0"},"suggested_fix":"Build the per-crate passes in run.sh's lint from the same `for toml in crates/*/clippy.toml` loop (excluding each such crate from the first pass), or move the loop into a helper in scripts/lib/ that both scripts call.","summary":"`just lint` under Bazel names clax-commands as the one crate with its own clippy.toml, while scripts/bazel-test.sh finds them by glob","tags":["review","review:deep-dive-2026-10-19","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/bazel-test.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T15:58:57.162507Z","id":"6dc3b28382d3fb217b648ef2a261edae83171a36365bedb7fe041bab6ff9ba0b","body":{"detail":"scripts/lib/build-system.sh:44-64 (_bazel_usable) uses a plain `bazel` only when `bazel --version` matches .bazelversion, because \"another version fails far from the cause\". scripts/bazel-test.sh:44 and scripts/bazel-sync.sh:99-102 pick `command -v bazelisk || command -v bazel` with no such check, so `just ci` (run.sh decides Bazel via build-system.sh, then calls bazel-test.sh) and a direct `scripts/bazel-test.sh` can run different binaries, and a mismatched distro bazel gets used by bazel-sync.sh and fails with lockfile or module errors that do not name the version.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":44},"end":{"line":49},"content_hash":"7995342c044599561bc20d21a46bf36b338bd50773ebf7f310e9002cd11893ab"},"suggested_fix":"Source scripts/lib/build-system.sh in bazel-test.sh and bazel-sync.sh and use `_bazel_usable`/$BAZEL (keeping BAZEL_SYNC_BAZEL as the test override), so all three pick Bazel the same way.","summary":"bazel-test.sh and bazel-sync.sh take any `bazel` on PATH, where scripts/lib/build-system.sh accepts a plain bazel only at the .bazelversion version","tags":["review","review:deep-dive-2026-10-19","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/bazel-test.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:00:47.488664Z","id":"df06584b5b7b1721b482eb43bea38fe2c67654bc5268cb2c663c0b37be493664","body":{"detail":"scripts/lib/build-system.sh:52-60 uses a plain bazel only when `bazel --version` equals .bazelversion. scripts/bazel-test.sh:44 is `bazel=\"$(command -v bazelisk || command -v bazel || true)\"` and scripts/bazel-sync.sh:99-102 the same (after BAZEL_SYNC_BAZEL), with no version check. With Bazelisk installed both pick it, so through `just ci` they agree; a direct `scripts/bazel-test.sh` or `just bazel-sync` with only a mismatched distro bazel uses it where run.sh would fall back to native. Suggestion kind fits.","kind":"comment","references":"6dc3b28382d3fb217b648ef2a261edae83171a36365bedb7fe041bab6ff9ba0b","summary":"confirmed: bazel-test.sh and bazel-sync.sh accept any bazel on PATH; build-system.sh checks the version","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/run.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:00:47.488851Z","id":"f3957a64f75a8ea05da271892c776986d2fc68a7ebce3e85ed81a61563a35f4f","body":{"detail":"scripts/run.sh:146-150 excludes `-//crates/clax-commands/...` and passes `//crates/clax-commands:clippy.toml` by name; scripts/bazel-test.sh:208-213 loops `for toml in crates/*/clippy.toml`. `ls crates/*/clippy.toml` prints only crates/clax-commands/clippy.toml, so they agree today and diverge when a second crate adds one.","kind":"comment","references":"7c6ad22aa8176cb7d53106c9502f00c016fdc89c6e42689834d3a72ba02d309b","summary":"confirmed: run.sh lint hard-codes clax-commands while bazel-test.sh globs crates/*/clippy.toml","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:00:47.489009Z","id":"eb0eba83e0c9500edb558999b6e9c3f52e688959ee8b5a7f60157ff80328517f","body":{"detail":"scripts/quality_gates.sh:23-31: `while ! mkdir \"$LOCK\"; do holder=$(cat \"$LOCK/pid\" ...); if [ -n \"$holder\" ] && ! kill -0 \"$holder\"; then rm -rf \"$LOCK\"; continue; fi; ... sleep 10; done; echo $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"`. Two waiters that both read the same dead PID can each `rm -rf \"$LOCK\"`, the second removing the lock the first just re-created. A run killed between `mkdir` and `echo $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"` leaves an empty holder, which the `[ -n \"$holder\" ]` guard never treats as stale.","kind":"comment","references":"8b67a515a0421d516a1342d690291a6fd323926418909f08f26d10cfcb657491","summary":"confirmed: stale-lock takeover races, and a PID-less lock is waited on forever","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:00:47.489323Z","id":"ccdb0be6921c2d1c6a7cf8612c6d6c71af98ca57d96f12574b68fbc0d36746f7","body":{"detail":"scripts/quality_gates.sh:252-263: `i=$((i + 1)); if [ \"$i\" -gt 9000 ]; then echo \"quality gates: gave up waiting for $1\"; return 1; fi; sleep 0.2`, and the comment above says \"or after 30 minutes\". The `.done` check at lines 256-258 already ends the wait when the awaited lane exits without marking (the lane's EXIT trap touches `.done`, line 243). AGENTS.md (\"Code\"): \"no wall-clock budgets in any check\".","kind":"comment","references":"ad18e90298d2d15379dc6f64ad4dcadc388175aaeda341efba964d6a0639b0e5","summary":"confirmed: await fails after 9000 x 0.2 s, a wall-clock budget AGENTS.md forbids","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/run.test.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:00:47.489785Z","id":"ee0f8f66a597af389b055ff5502aa3951cda5b7b0cb0401555c6aba8bfa529ae","body":{"detail":"scripts/run.test.sh:228 `links=\"$(stat -f %l \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || stat -c %h \"$f\")\"`; GNU stat -f is file-system mode where %l is max filename length (https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/stat-invocation.html). scripts/BUILD.bazel:224-226 defines run_choice; .github/workflows/ci.yml:9 runs-on ubuntu-latest and :43 runs scripts/bazel-test.sh --ci. `gh run view 39266520838 --log-failed | grep FAIL` prints `FAIL: //scripts:run_choice (Exit 1)` and `FAIL: web: copies, writable, one link each`. scripts/test-extension-signing.sh:104 and scripts/test-dev.sh:48 put GNU `-c` first.","kind":"comment","references":"f4b54e1e9ce4bdb91e2150c8f4250d18f87f175b52d7c6e23b93120668c34c47","summary":"confirmed: `stat -f %l` first makes //scripts:run_choice fail on Linux CI","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/run.test.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:57:10.385698Z","id":"de9fe1caf67cdf0d2e219686adb0b0caa6984d38fb9927a397e56c4501b0604b","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:42c6250d3","summary":"Fixed: link count uses find -links 1; bazelisk test //scripts:run_choice passes; verified in ubuntu:24.04 that find -links 1 works where stat -f %l misreads.","supersedes":"f4b54e1e9ce4bdb91e2150c8f4250d18f87f175b52d7c6e23b93120668c34c47","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:57:10.536795Z","id":"ab6736a7310639f15714f6be907f1e0035078749a8fb9881300b0053ea9aefd4","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:2bf60a87e","summary":"Fixed: the checkout lock is a flock(2) (scripts/lib/checkout-lock.sh), atomic with no stale takeover; bazelisk test //scripts:checkout_lock passes (kill -9 release, six contenders never overlap).","supersedes":"8b67a515a0421d516a1342d690291a6fd323926418909f08f26d10cfcb657491","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/quality_gates.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:57:10.928581Z","id":"cbd0e15f3ee046447c184f34def84757d35474577b910da7ae0d9b2beeaaf4b6","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c3844a30c","summary":"Fixed: the 9000-poll limit in await is removed; await returns when the lane's .done appears.","supersedes":"ad18e90298d2d15379dc6f64ad4dcadc388175aaeda341efba964d6a0639b0e5","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/bazel-test.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:57:12.096629Z","id":"7f001ead11be38da4b50f435297d159f95c88726f141a57533a8c34d6d7d4451","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:cdd021d8f","summary":"Fixed: bazel-test.sh and bazel-sync.sh pick Bazel through scripts/lib/build-system.sh _bazel_usable; scripts/bazel-sync.test.sh wrong-version case passes.","supersedes":"6dc3b28382d3fb217b648ef2a261edae83171a36365bedb7fe041bab6ff9ba0b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/run.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-19T16:57:12.371419Z","id":"1996cf5cfa6104a28ddcf20527339ee3fdbf8ceab72597f3a6e1f098faab4ed6","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e5668ae1c","summary":"Fixed: run.sh finds crates/*/clippy.toml by glob; bazelisk test //scripts:run_choice case 'lint: each crate's own clippy.toml, found by glob' passes (failed before).","supersedes":"7c6ad22aa8176cb7d53106c9502f00c016fdc89c6e42689834d3a72ba02d309b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:39187025-0a9f-4b13-8d2e-a1ea08e924ac"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/release.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T01:37:25.205789Z","id":"bec0532206b6cefb87b9eb93687ab6ec947a0fbeb45b6cdc5b8e421f78570079","body":{"detail":"is_signed_commit and is_signed_tag look for a gpgsig header or a SIGNATURE block. git verify-commit/verify-tag would need gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile for SSH signing, which the owner's config may not set, so the presence check is what keeps an unsigned commit or tag from being pushed. A signature by the wrong key would pass.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":123},"end":{"line":124},"content_hash":"5730bf23787f48c7ab971a356cfae8c05e7cbcd85b8cac921374d6a7f4e69eaf"},"suggested_fix":"Verify with git verify-commit/verify-tag when gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile (or a GPG keyring) is configured, and fall back to the presence check otherwise.","summary":"Signature checks before a push test that a signature is present, not that it verifies","tags":["session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/release.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T01:37:25.205875Z","id":"6dde1fb4be9fc559ca154216904cadf2d67a0e411d8b702f679a45fb584c29b8","body":{"detail":"The waits poll gh run view every CLAX_RELEASE_POLL_SECONDS, at most CLAX_RELEASE_MAX_POLLS times, instead of gh run watch: gh run watch has no timeout of its own (macOS has no timeout(1)), and the poll interval can be zero in scripts/test-release-script.sh. gh run watch would show job-level progress.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":350},"end":{"line":380},"content_hash":"678a9a096111d177ba44699b939a54c0e4fda1670f12a2db635ea8d1bfecb377"},"summary":"gh run watch for the workflow waits","tags":["revisit:the owner wants job-level progress during the waits","session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/release.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T01:37:25.205914Z","id":"a6d9af55fa2a3989f5847ef21c00a9fbe6776f1f39453d66ac7c885110e6a9d6","body":{"detail":"gh workflow run prints no run ID. A dispatch by someone else of publish-pi.yml in the same seconds could be taken for this one; a crash between the dispatch and writing target/release/X.Y.Z/publish-pi-run makes the next run dispatch again, which .github/workflows/publish-pi.yml refuses once npm has the version. Accepted: publish-pi is dispatched by hand, by the owner, rarely.","kind":"waiver","span":{"start":{"line":433},"end":{"line":451},"content_hash":"c9f62387e471280a97633263891a099d277aac441e43bb08727924c87a1973c8"},"summary":"The Publish Pi package run is identified as the first new workflow_dispatch run on main after the dispatch","tags":["session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/release.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T01:37:25.205945Z","id":"85c0f890b0094da2c8887b85ce193b9b3716723c20eac285f04d1c0e5d80ceb3","body":{"detail":"Resuming after a failed just ci, a fix made in the work tree is committed with git commit -S -a in the release (or pin) commit, as the manual procedure's git commit -am did. step_5 and step_11 list the files they commit. A fix committed separately would make main ahead of origin/main by a commit that is not the release's, which preflight refuses.","kind":"waiver","span":{"start":{"line":252},"end":{"line":262},"content_hash":"7d89c57ec7f706cacfcc8efdcb68f043c5dbfd7a5f4684e9c8bf4cdb24a02913"},"summary":"While steps 2-4 or 9-10 are pending, every modified tracked file goes into the release or pin commit","tags":["session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"scripts/test-release-script.sh","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-20T01:37:25.205989Z","id":"1f155a8a31c6a922f139f5b355378d711b54c77ad288004baf1a20e29cf79f96","body":{"detail":"find_run, publish_runs, run_state and release_has_sums in scripts/release.sh pass --json/--jq to gh; the fake ignores both and prints the filtered text. A wrong field name or jq expression would pass this test and fail against GitHub.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":60},"end":{"line":104},"content_hash":"aa1f9ab4aae7b49e6282d91e45165a2048cb8c978db5466342ac6a95767c74b9"},"suggested_fix":"Have the fake print JSON and run the --jq expression through jq (or gh's own --jq on a fixture) where jq is installed.","summary":"The fake gh prints what each --jq expression would, so release.sh's jq expressions are not tested","tags":["session:claude-code:d1f51555-8e8f-4c11-acd6-673f773ac34d"]}}
